#{[17592186350765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350765 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "21188bb3-7c29-56d5-bce0-52b90aee9ec7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350983 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2ba48f4-bfb9-53b1-b63c-347212329c4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351004 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "adc8c03c-2db7-556e-a6d7-5bf3ef6dc447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351128 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef510875-8ddb-5754-836a-118c96470baf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $U=\\{-\\pi ,\\pi \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ade80e9-fb10-5f67-9c65-ab5d8d98adbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351158 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $U,$ we list out every combination of elements within $U.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/b0/b0e483a2d10cba17fb2bd3bf0e78d7b4d2fc07b22dbb5c492564b998bbfce6fc.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{-\\pi \\},\\{\\pi \\},\\{-\\pi ,\\pi \\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$  be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $D$  be the the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0.$ What is $C∖D?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1076728a-78e3-5cde-ae2f-0952fd39a682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351176 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiples of $10$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nC=\\{10,20,30,40,50,60,70,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nD=\\{5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $C∖D$ is the *difference* of $C$ and $D,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $C$ but *not* in $D.$\n\nThe elements of $C$ that are also in $D$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $10$). So, we must remove these elements from $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∖D & =C-D \\\\  & =\\{10,20,30,40,50,60,70,…\\}-\\{5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,…\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1071 "If $U=\\{1,2,3,…,10\\}$ is the universal set and $A=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\},$ then the complement of $A$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35334f88-cecb-5c05-827d-5c2f77f4118a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351258 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are $1,$ $3,$ $5,$ $7,$ and $9.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1071 "If $U=\\{-20,-18,-16,-14,…,20\\}$ is the universal set and $A=\\{-20,-16,-12,-8,…,20\\},$ then $\\overset{―}{A}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99c47e36-6326-59cd-8a70-c88572cc5f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351259 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the even integers $-20$ through $20,$ while the set $A$ consists of the multiples of $4$ from $-20$ through $20.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are the even integers from $-18$ through $18$ that are not multiples of $4.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A}=\\{-18,-14,-10,-6,-2,2,6,10,14,18\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3b539190899ca580528fdbf7b5d3ef9925ade29f97f4b2f96b0f1394743f40a8.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S=\\{p,a,s,w,o,r,d\\}$ and $T=\\{p,o,r,t,e,l\\},$ find $T∖S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "065e9298-e9b9-5450-a8e3-c9e554981a9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351347 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference $T∖S$ consists of all the elements that are in $T$ but not $S.$ To represent $T∖S,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in $T$ but not $S.$\n\n![](images/c7/c718918667c5076f8696ea98f453ff17f005d5f7cace87bd863be309fac28d72.png)\n\nThe elements $t,e,$ and $l$ are in $T$ but not $S.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nT∖S=\\{t,e,l\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$  be the set of all multiples of $3$ greater than $0$ and let $B$  be the set of all multiples of $4$ greater than $0.$ What is $B-A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08cc3802-c796-53b6-b40f-b3be60f49c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351367 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiples of $3$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA=\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB=\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $B-A$ is the *difference* of $B$ and $A,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $B$ but *not* in $A.$\n\nThe elements of $B$ that are also in $A$ are $12,24,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $12$). So, we must remove these elements from $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-A & =\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,…\\}-\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{4,8,16,20,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351832 #edb/ref 1080 "∅" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dbc40ae-f26c-5818-bfd4-bddc3ecd358a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353842 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0ea4e7ef-a234-58ac-9208-db95f3628325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186351832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354073 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "912b3d79-97df-5c30-a3f5-eec985f87656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354100 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2f9adc9-b73b-54e9-ac8c-1d5f98dfb7ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/02/02dabe7099c201f717885a05842e0cb8543bd01cccf0d6c52147f674cde15dce.png)\n\nLet $U=\\{a,b,c,20,40\\}$ be the universal set and let $R=\\{b,20,40\\}.$ What is the complement of $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4434ccc8-02d5-5a76-9c36-2b57d531d580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354480 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $R,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{R},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $R.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{R},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $R,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/79/79900f22c35570b35cf10664768ecf4460a7953ddf7f91dac1893b3ce81339bf.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $a$ and $c.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $R.$ Therefore, the complement of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{R}=\\{a,c\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1071 "If $U=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}$ is the universal set and $B=\\{2,6,10\\},$ then the complement of $B$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04125aec-6df3-511f-bf67-6f782ff92bbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354481 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $4,$ $8,$   and $12.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{B}=\\{4,8,12\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/96962d11526f30beee45b85aac92808518352d7b79b4b4f2e4a7351ddd9fe17e.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S=\\{5,7,9\\}$ and $T=\\{0,1,3\\},$find $S∖T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af7efa2c-3ffa-5821-9cbc-8c2275ebe689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354497 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference $S∖T$ consists of all the elements that are in $S$ but not $T.$ To represent $S∖T,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in $S$ but not $T.$\n\n![](images/be/be1caefc99bea5d4dde0ba272a208dd8f587156029ce37df483e40cd5f6121d2.png)\n\nAll elements of $S$ are not in $T.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nS∖T=\\{5,7,9\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/3905dacc3977857933a48ced329fc245b357f732aec520e67998efc0b5d7cdab.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9a72356-7e05-5f10-b6ac-a2418c3b4fa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399685 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $x+2y=-8.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x-4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x-4,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+2y\\ge -8.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399686 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fce68956-d8ec-5373-80c1-0576c6b35476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10f47672-b02f-5835-a498-94f2d2a69a14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400380 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and  $y$,  the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+8y=6 \\\\ 4x+y=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8y=6 \\\\ 4x+y=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400381 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f8ada74-f43b-5b8f-a0b1-562915c30921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 4 & -\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{2}{3} & 8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{3} & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0c417e1-432e-591b-889d-c01227fedeb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400387 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$, $y$, and $z,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0x-8y+4z=-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ 0x+5y+\\frac{2}{3}z=8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3}y+4z=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy simplifying, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8y+4z=-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ 5y+\\frac{2}{3}z=8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3}y+4z=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a53c95c7-2729-5853-ad5b-f91dbb44a5b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1162f6f-3b06-5edc-926f-fe3ac4ca244f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400485 #edb/ref 1074 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+5y=9 \\\\ x+0y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}5y=9 \\\\ x=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400486 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0c4d849c-e24c-5150-9286-220ece393a3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a7/a740aafbb9f74e2681506d3027f848fa92e7eea2bac3e08ccaf3fc7e0acf8fa1.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown in the graph above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cb8229e-2a6a-582b-a6c3-627b88cf6816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401928 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "210acf01-110f-53da-bf3c-8725abe1a7d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/75a7f3bfae55a97fbc0c05e2e0356d21de6ceabcc82ac3e1c965838a636f5ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9187138f-ae2f-5a56-952a-6135b67724bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403037 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $y=3x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3x+1.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6391aa03-c66c-510d-8de3-714552c986fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ba/ba01cf5268f2bd62835c925a5eb793c0d98663948899c9e9aba2eeacd37ff30a.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d48a81ca-ca4e-5354-bd11-f73315ba7d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403044 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $y=2x+6.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<2x+6.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=5.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403045 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "057029ec-da64-5909-af01-3dd51581c107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b8c1d5902a76a68e5f8358d4c3668e1be184ec7241184df4ff1363d41995f3aa.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "262609c0-5557-5285-b43c-f82fdb8825a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404461 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $x+y=3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+3,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+y>3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict.  Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404462 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8c5be21f-60e7-597b-83dc-3c20339aa469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e863789ff989ee1cb3d3fe6c22fe0d4daa1dac76d6fbfbebdad801006ca4c969.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66b0d8c5-2487-53cc-b840-b06edcc12d56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404468 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the line $y=4x+3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<4x+3.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404469 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 